          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Guides",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Can You Stain Composite Decking",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/can-you-stain-composite-decking/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you stain composite decking?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not in the way you stain wood. Stain works by soaking into wood fibres, and composite has no fibres available to soak into — particularly capped composite, which wears a solid polymer shell. What some people mean by staining composite is painting it with a solid-colour acrylic coating, and that is possible on older uncapped boards with heavy preparation. On capped composite it will not bond reliably and should not be attempted.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can Trex decking be stained?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Trex does not permit it and it does not work. Every Trex line made since 2010 is capped — sealed in a polyethylene shell that no stain can penetrate — and Trex states plainly that its products are not to be painted or stained. Doing so voids the fade-and-stain warranty, which on Transcend runs 25 years and is a large part of what you paid for.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does staining or painting composite decking void the warranty?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, on effectively every major brand. Trex, TimberTech, Fiberon and Deckorators all exclude coated boards from their fade, stain and structural warranties. If your decking is still inside its warranty period, this alone is usually reason enough not to coat it, since a warranty replacement is worth far more than a paint job.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What kind of paint or stain works on composite decking?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Only a 100% acrylic latex solid-colour product rated for exterior use — either a dedicated composite deck paint or a solid-colour acrylic deck stain. Oil-based stains, penetrating stains, semi-transparent stains and any product that relies on absorption will not work at all. Acrylic remains flexible enough to move with the boards, which expand and contract more than wood does.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long does paint last on composite decking?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Two to four years on a well-prepared uncapped board, less on walkways. Composite moves seasonally more than wood and offers a slick surface for a coating to grip, so the failure mode is peeling rather than fading. Once coated, the deck is on a repaint cycle permanently — there is no going back to bare composite.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How can you tell if composite decking is capped or uncapped?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Look at a cut end or the underside. Capped boards show a distinct outer layer of a different colour or texture, usually a smooth shell over a rougher core, and the surface feels slick and slightly plasticky. Uncapped boards look and feel the same all the way through, are more matt, and tend to be pre-2010 product. Age is a good proxy: almost everything sold since roughly 2013 is capped.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do you restore faded composite decking without painting it?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Deep cleaning removes most of what looks like fading, because much of it is a film of pollen, dirt and mildew rather than lost colour. Use a composite-safe cleaner with a soft brush and rinse thoroughly. Genuine UV fading is permanent, but it plateaus after the first year or two, so an older deck is unlikely to fade much further.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you change the colour of composite decking?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not durably. Painting is the only mechanism and it comes with a two-to-four-year repaint cycle, a voided warranty and a surface that no longer looks like composite. If the colour is genuinely wrong, replacing boards on a high-visibility area, or living with it, are usually the better answers.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you stain composite railings if you leave the boards alone?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The same rules apply — capped railings cannot be coated reliably, uncapped ones can be painted with acrylic. Railings are actually the better candidate of the two, because they take no foot traffic and shed water, so a coating on a railing routinely lasts twice as long as the same coating on the deck floor.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
