    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#short-answer" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. The Short Answer</a>
          <a href="#capped" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Capped vs Uncapped</a>
          <a href="#trex" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. What Trex and the Others Say</a>
          <a href="#warranty" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. The Warranty Problem</a>
          <a href="#if-you-must" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. If You Are Painting Anyway</a>
          <a href="#lifespan" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. How Long It Lasts</a>
          <a href="#alternatives" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Better Alternatives</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Stain is not a colour you apply to a surface. It is a liquid that soaks into wood fibres and dries inside them. Composite decking has nothing for it to soak into, which is why this question has a different answer from every other staining question on this site.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Verdict</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">You cannot stain composite decking. You can, on older uncapped boards only, paint it with a solid acrylic — and doing so voids the warranty and starts a repaint cycle that never ends.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">On capped composite, which is essentially everything sold in the last fifteen years, no coating bonds reliably to the polymer shell. The honest recommendation there is to clean it properly and leave it alone.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            It is worth separating two things people mean by this question. Some are asking whether a deck stain can be applied to composite the way it is applied to cedar, to darken the grain and repel water. That is simply not how the material works — there is no absorption, so the stain sits wet on the surface until it is walked into the house.
          </p>

          <p>
            Others are asking whether a faded or blotchy composite deck can be given a new colour. That is a paint question rather than a stain question, and it has a real if unattractive answer, which the rest of this guide covers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="capped" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Capped vs Uncapped: The Only Distinction That Matters
          </h2>

          <p>
            Composite decking comes in two generations, and they behave completely differently under a coating.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Uncapped</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Capped</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Era</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly pre-2010</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2010 onward, universal by 2013</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Surface</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Same material throughout, matt, slightly porous</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Polymer shell over a wood-flour core</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Accepts paint?</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, with heavy prep and acrylic only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No — nothing bonds reliably</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Accepts stain?</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical complaint</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fading, mould staining, chalky surface</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Colour is wrong, or looks tired</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            To tell which you have, look at a cut end or crouch under the deck and look at the underside of a board. A capped board shows an obvious outer layer, usually smoother and often a slightly different shade than the core, and the walking surface feels slick under a fingernail. An uncapped board is uniform all the way through and feels more like a dense, matt fibreboard.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you know when the deck was built, that settles it almost as well. Anything installed since about 2013 is capped. Anything from the early 2000s — the era of Trex Accents, ChoiceDek and the first Fiberon boards — is almost certainly not, and those are the decks that turn up faded and chalky and prompt this question in the first place.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="trex" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Trex and the Other Manufacturers Say
          </h2>

          <p>
            The manufacturers are unusually consistent on this point, which is not something you can say about most product guidance.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Trex</strong> states that its decking is not to be painted or stained, and that doing so voids the warranty. Every current line — Transcend, Select, Enhance — is capped.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>TimberTech and AZEK</strong> take the same position on their capped composite and PVC products, and their fade-and-stain warranties exclude coated boards.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Fiberon</strong> likewise advises against coatings on capped product and notes that any coating applied becomes the homeowner's ongoing maintenance responsibility.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Older uncapped lines</strong> are the exception, and even then the guidance is permissive rather than encouraging — the manufacturers do not recommend it, they simply acknowledge it is possible.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            This is not the usual manufacturer caution about liability. The physics genuinely does not favour you. A capped board's shell is engineered specifically to shed everything — mould, wine, barbecue grease and, inconveniently, paint. A coating on it has nothing to key into, so it holds by surface tension until the first freeze or the first patio chair drags across it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Warranty Problem
          </h2>

          <p>
            Composite decking is sold on its warranty as much as on its looks. Trex Transcend carries a 25-year limited fade-and-stain warranty; most capped competitors are in the same territory. That warranty is a meaningful part of what the boards cost, and coating them forfeits it entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical arithmetic is worth spelling out. If a capped deck is fading unevenly inside its warranty period, the manufacturer may replace boards. Paint it first and you have converted a possible free replacement into a permanent maintenance obligation, for a coating that will start peeling in two or three years. On a deck still inside its warranty, that is close to the worst available trade.
          </p>

          <p>
            An old uncapped deck well outside its warranty is a different situation, and that is where painting can be defensible — the warranty is gone anyway, the boards look chalky, and the alternative is replacement at ten times the cost.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="if-you-must" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. If You Are Painting an Uncapped Deck Anyway
          </h2>

          <p>
            Assume the deck is uncapped, out of warranty, and you have accepted that this is now a repainted deck forever. The process that gives the best odds:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clean Far Harder Than You Think</h3>
          <p>
            Composite holds a chalky oxidised film and a surprising amount of embedded mould, and paint will not stick to either. Use a composite-safe deck cleaner with a stiff nylon brush, work it in, and let it dwell for the full recommended time. Rinse thoroughly and repeat wherever the surface still feels powdery under a dry hand. Our <a href="/how-to-clean-a-deck/">deck cleaning guide</a> covers the method and the dwell times.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Scuff, Do Not Sand</h3>
          <p>
            A light pass with 60 to 80 grit on the walking surfaces gives the coating something to grip, but the aim is to dull the sheen, not to remove material. Sanding hard generates a plastic-and-wood dust that clogs paper instantly and can leave visible swirl marks under a solid colour. Sweep and wipe every trace of dust afterwards.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use 100% Acrylic, Solid Colour, Nothing Else</h3>
          <p>
            Either a dedicated composite deck coating or a solid-colour acrylic deck stain. Acrylic keeps enough flexibility to survive the seasonal movement of composite, which is greater than wood's. Oil-based products, penetrating stains and anything semi-transparent are non-starters — they depend on absorption that is not there. Our <a href="/best-deck-paint/">deck paint picks</a> cover the acrylics that hold up outdoors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Test One Board First</h3>
          <p>
            Coat a section in an out-of-the-way corner, let it cure for a full week, then attack it — drag a chair across it, scrub it, pick at an edge with a fingernail. A coating that fails this test on one board will fail on the whole deck, just more slowly and more expensively. This single step saves more composite deck owners from a bad weekend than any other advice here.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Two Thin Coats, and Respect the Cure</h3>
          <p>
            Thin coats bond better and bridge less. Give the first coat its full recoat window, and keep furniture off for a week rather than the 24 to 48 hours the can suggests — a coating on a slick substrate needs every day of cure it can get before anything heavy sits on it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lifespan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Long It Actually Lasts
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two to four years on the deck floor of a well-prepared uncapped board, and less than that in the walkways and around the door. Railings and vertical faces do considerably better — four to six years is realistic — because they take no traffic and shed water.
          </p>

          <p>
            The failure mode is peeling, not fading, and that is the important difference from staining wood. A penetrating stain on cedar wears away evenly and is renewed with a wash and a coat. A film on composite lifts in flakes, usually starting at board edges and around fasteners, and every renewal from then on means scraping and sanding the failed areas back to a sound edge.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is the commitment nobody mentions at the paint counter. The first coat is a weekend. Every coat after it is a weekend plus a scraping job, on a rising schedule, permanently. It is worth being certain the deck's appearance genuinely warrants that before opening the first can.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What to Do Instead
          </h2>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Deep clean first, and judge afterwards.</strong> A great deal of what reads as fading is a film of pollen, dirt and mildew. A proper cleaning brings back more colour than most owners expect, and it costs $25.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Treat mould as mould.</strong> Black spotting on composite is usually surface mould feeding on debris in the embossed grain, not damage to the board. An oxygenated cleaner and a brush removes it.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Replace the boards that show.</strong> Swapping a dozen boards in the main sightline is often cheaper over ten years than committing an entire deck to a repaint cycle.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Flip the boards, where the profile allows.</strong> Some older uncapped boards are finished on both faces, and the underside has spent fifteen years out of the sun.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Claim under the warranty while you still can.</strong> If the deck is capped and inside its fade warranty, photograph it and call the manufacturer before you do anything else at all.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Accept the plateau.</strong> Composite fades fastest in its first year or two and then largely stops. A ten-year-old board is close to the colour it will be at twenty.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            And if the deck is wood rather than composite and you arrived here by accident, the answer is far more encouraging — wood takes stain readily, and our guide to <a href="/how-to-stain-a-deck/">staining a deck</a> covers the whole job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">The Mixed-Material Deck</h2>
        <p>
          Plenty of decks are composite boards on a wood frame with wood or aluminium railings, and that combination is where the question gets practical rather than theoretical. Stain the wood elements normally and leave the composite alone. Mask the composite boards properly while you do it — stain that dries on a capped surface does not soak in, but it does leave a film that has to be scrubbed off before it sets.
        </p>
        <p>
          Wipe any overspray or drips off composite within a few minutes with a rag and the cleaner recommended for the stain. Left overnight, an oil-based stain on a capped board becomes a genuinely stubborn removal job, and aggressive scrubbing can dull the cap permanently.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying Wood Stain to Composite</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A penetrating stain has nothing to penetrate. It stays wet on the surface, tracks into the house on shoes, then dries as a patchy film that has to be removed with a stripper — on a material some strippers will damage. There is no version of this that ends well.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting Capped Boards</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The polymer shell is engineered to shed liquids, and paint is a liquid. It may look fine for a season, then lift in sheets from the traffic areas — and the deck now needs scraping rather than simply cleaning. Test on a hidden board and be honest about the result.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pressure Washing on a High Setting</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Composite erodes under a narrow tip far more easily than wood, and the marks are permanent because there is no grain to sand back. Most manufacturers cap it at around 1,500 psi with a wide fan held well back. A brush and cleaner is the safer tool.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating Before Claiming the Warranty</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fade-and-stain warranties on capped composite run 25 years and cover exactly the complaint that prompts most people to paint. Coating the deck forfeits it permanently. Photograph the fading and call the manufacturer first — it costs a phone call.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Cure Before Replacing Furniture</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A film on a slick substrate needs a full week before anything heavy sits on it, whatever the can says. Chair feet placed on a two-day-old coating leave permanent marks and start the first peel, usually right where everybody looks.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you stain composite decking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in the way you stain wood. Stain soaks into wood fibres and composite has none available, particularly capped composite with its polymer shell. What people usually mean is painting with a solid acrylic, which is possible on older uncapped boards with heavy prep — and not on capped ones.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can Trex decking be stained?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Every Trex line since 2010 is capped in a polyethylene shell that stain cannot penetrate, and Trex states its products are not to be painted or stained. Doing it voids the fade-and-stain warranty, which runs 25 years on Transcend and is a large part of what the boards cost.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does staining or painting composite void the warranty?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, on effectively every major brand — Trex, TimberTech, Fiberon and Deckorators all exclude coated boards. If the decking is still inside its warranty period, that alone is usually reason enough not to coat it, since a replacement claim is worth far more than a paint job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What kind of paint or stain works on composite?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only a 100% acrylic latex solid-colour exterior product — a composite deck paint or a solid-colour acrylic deck stain. Oil-based, penetrating and semi-transparent products rely on absorption and will not work. Acrylic stays flexible enough for composite's seasonal movement.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does paint last on composite decking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Two to four years on a well-prepared uncapped board, less in walkways. Composite moves more than wood and gives a coating little to grip, so the failure mode is peeling rather than fading. Once coated, the deck is on a repaint cycle permanently.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How can you tell if your composite is capped?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look at a cut end or the underside. Capped boards show a distinct outer layer over a rougher core and feel slick. Uncapped boards are uniform through and more matt. Age is a good proxy — almost everything sold since about 2013 is capped.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you restore faded composite without painting it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Deep clean it — much of what looks like fading is pollen, dirt and mildew rather than lost colour. Use a composite-safe cleaner with a soft brush and rinse well. True UV fading is permanent but plateaus after the first year or two, so an older deck will not fade much further.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you stain composite railings only?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The same rules apply, but railings are the better candidate — no foot traffic and they shed water, so a coating there routinely lasts twice as long as the same coating on the deck floor. Capped railings still cannot be coated reliably.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

