        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Composite decking cannot be stained</strong>, because stain works by absorption and there is nothing there to absorb it. Capped boards — everything Trex has made since 2010, and effectively all composite sold since 2013 — cannot reliably be painted either. The shell is designed to shed exactly what you are trying to apply.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Older uncapped boards can be painted with a 100% acrylic solid colour after a hard clean and a light scuff, and it looks good for two to four years. But it voids the warranty, it fails by peeling rather than wearing, and every renewal from then on includes scraping. Test one board and leave it a week before committing the deck.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Before any of that, clean it properly. A large share of composite decks that look faded are simply dirty, and a $25 cleaner with a stiff brush settles the question one way or the other in an afternoon — without spending the warranty to find out.
          </p>
        </div>


